{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Boards — who builds and who uses Shopware 6 extensions — extdir{% endblock %}
{% block canonical %}<link rel="canonical" href="{{ url('boards') }}">{% endblock %}
{% block meta_description %}Which vendors maintain the most open-source Shopware 6 extensions, which ship the most releases, and which extensions are installed most — each board with the rule that produced it.{% endblock %}
{% block og_title %}Who builds and who uses open-source Shopware 6 extensions{% endblock %}
{% block og_description %}Six leaderboards over the whole indexed catalogue. Every board publishes the expression behind it, and none of them affects how extensions are ranked.{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
<div class="wrap page">
    <div class="page-head">
        <h1>Boards</h1>
        <p>
            Six leaderboards over the whole indexed catalogue. Each one prints the rule that
            produced it, so any of them can be checked rather than trusted.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
        <h2>Who builds the ecosystem</h2>
        <p>
            “Most contributing” has at least three honest meanings, and they disagree. The vendor
            that ships the most releases maintains a third as many extensions as the vendor with
            the most extensions. Rather than pick one meaning and present it as the answer, all
            three are here, side by side, with no winner declared.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="board-grid">
        {% for board in vendorBoards %}
            {% include 'pages/_board.html.twig' with {board: board} only %}
        {% endfor %}
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
        <h2>What people install</h2>
        <p>
            Two of these boards count Packagist installs. Read them together, not separately: a
            lifetime total only ever accumulates, so it rewards age as much as use and an
            extension nobody has installed since 2021 can still sit near the top of it. The
            thirty-day board cannot say that.
        </p>
        <p class="notice notice--info">
            <strong>{{ coverage.measured }} of {{ coverage.total }} listed extensions are on Packagist.</strong>
            The rest are published only on a forge, so no install count exists for them at all.
            Absence from these two boards means unmeasured, never unpopular — and the extensions it
            affects are disproportionately the agency-built ones this directory went looking for.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="board-grid">
        {% for board in extensionBoards %}
            {% include 'pages/_board.html.twig' with {board: board} only %}
        {% endfor %}
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
        <h2>None of this affects ranking</h2>
        <p>
            Installs and stars appear on this page and nowhere in the score. That is deliberate and
            long-standing: in this ecosystem popularity is a misleading signal, because an
            excellent extension built by a German agency for its own clients may have a dozen
            stars while an abandoned experiment has hundreds. Ranking on it would bury precisely
            the extensions merchants most need to find.
        </p>
        <p>
            So the numbers here are shown, and the order of the catalogue is decided by
            <a href="{{ path('ranking') }}">the published formula</a> — compatibility, maintenance,
            the quality of the compatibility claim, licence clarity, responsiveness and recency.
            No board on this page feeds into it.
        </p>
        <p>
            There is also no manual placement anywhere in this feature: no curated list, no
            override table, no configuration naming a vendor. The only way onto a board is to
            satisfy the rule printed under it. That includes the vendor operated by this
            directory’s own maintainer, which appears on any board it qualifies for and always
            carries a disclosure chip when it does.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
{% endblock %}
